        "tekst": "**Social divisions** are differences made socially significant; **social stratification** ranks categories of people in a hierarchy of money, power and prestige (the Titanic: more than 60 per cent of first class survived, 24 per cent of third class). Five principles: a feature of society, persists over generations, universal but variable, involves beliefs, creates shared identities. **Intersectionality**. **Young's five processes**: exclusion, exploitation, powerlessness, cultural imperialism, violence.\n\n**Closed and open systems**: slavery (modern slavery: Bales, about 27 million), estates, **caste** (occupation, endogamy, segregation, cultural beliefs), and **class**; **status consistency**; **structural mobility**; Japan (burakumin, 1871) and Russia (power-based elites; Figure 8.1: the Russian Federation about 10:1). **Ideology** justifies stratification (Plato, Marx; from God's will to merit).\n\n**Explanations**: **Davis and Moore** (functional importance, meritocracy) with Tumin's criticisms; **Marx** (conflict over the means of production), **Dahrendorf's** four reasons why no revolution came and the neo-Marxist reply; **Weber's** class, status and power and socioeconomic status. Technology and the **Kuznets curve**; the **Bell Curve** debate; the bottom billions and the **matrix of inequalities**."
